EXPERIMENT - 1
WINDOWS DEFENDER FIREWALL
==================================================

AIM

To explore the functionality of Windows Defender Firewall and understand how it controls network traffic and protects a Windows system from unauthorized access.

DESCRIPTION

Windows Defender Firewall is a built-in security feature in Windows OS that monitors and regulates incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predefined security rules. This experiment demonstrates how enabling, disabling, and customizing firewall rules affects the behavior of applications and network connectivity.

PROCEDURE

1. Check Firewall Status

   1. Open Control Panel → System and Security → Windows Defender Firewall.
   2. Observe the firewall status (On/Off) for different network profiles (Private, Public).

2. Enable/Disable the Firewall

   1. Click Turn Windows Defender Firewall on or off.
   2. Disable the firewall temporarily and try accessing the internet or a network resource.
   3. Re-enable it afterward.

3. Create an Inbound Rule

   1. Open Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security.
   2. Go to Inbound Rules → New Rule.
   3. Choose Program and browse to an installed application (e.g., Chrome).
   4. Select Allow the connection, specify profiles, and finish setup.

4. Test Application Behavior

   1. Run the selected application.
   2. Observe whether it can access the internet or local network depending on the rule.

5. Block an Application

   1. Repeat the same steps as above but choose Block the connection.
   2. Observe changes in connectivity.

6. Reset Rules

   1. Go to Action → Restore Default Policy to reset all custom rules.

OBSERVATION

Scenario                          Application Behavior

Firewall Enabled + Allow Rule     Application runs normally with full connectivity.

Firewall Enabled + Block Rule     Application is blocked from accessing the network.

Firewall Disabled                 No restrictions; all applications have connectivity.

• Public networks apply stricter rules.
• Custom rules give precise control over app behavior and ports.

RESULT

The experiment successfully demonstrates how Windows Defender Firewall controls the flow of network traffic. Through rule management, users can enhance their system’s security by allowing only trusted applications and blocking potential threats.

