EXPERIMENT 4
NETWORK SCANNING USING IP SCANNER
==================================================

AIM

To demonstrate how an IP scanner tool can be used to detect active devices on a local network and analyze their connectivity status.

DESCRIPTION

An IP scanner is a network utility that scans a range of IP addresses to identify which hosts are active and reachable. It helps network administrators monitor devices, detect unauthorized access, and troubleshoot connectivity issues. This experiment uses an IP scanner (such as Angry IP Scanner or Advanced IP Scanner) to scan a subnet and record the status of connected devices.

PROCEDURE

1. Install the IP scanner tool

   1. Download and install Angry IP Scanner or Advanced IP Scanner from their official websites.

2. Launch the application

   1. Open the IP scanner tool on your computer.

3. Set the IP range

   1. Enter the IP address range to be scanned (e.g., 192.168.0.1 to 192.168.0.254).

4. Start the scan

   1. Click the "Start" or "Scan" button to begin scanning the network.

5. Observe the results

   1. Wait for the scan to complete. The tool will display a list of active IP addresses, host names, MAC addresses, and open ports.

6. Save or export the results

   1. Use the export feature to save the scan results to a file for documentation or analysis.

OUTPUT

The IP scanner successfully identifies all active devices within the specified IP range. The output includes IP addresses, host names, MAC addresses, and port status. This data can be used to verify network health, detect unknown devices, and assist in network troubleshooting.


