EXPERIMENT 5
NETWORK SCANNING AND PORT DISCOVERY USING NMAP
==================================================

AIM

To perform network scanning using Nmap in order to discover active hosts, open ports, and running services on a target system or network.

DESCRIPTION

Nmap is a command-line tool used by network administrators and cyber security professionals to map networks, identify devices, and detect vulnerabilities. It can perform host discovery, port scanning, service detection, and OS fingerprinting. This experiment demonstrates how to use Nmap to analyze a target IP or subnet and interpret the results for network diagnostics and security assessment.

PROCEDURE

1. Install Nmap

   • Download and install Nmap from https://nmap.org.
   • On Windows, use the installer; on Linux/macOS, install via package manager (sudo apt install nmap or brew install nmap).

2. Open Terminal or Command Prompt

   • Launch your terminal or command prompt with administrative privileges.

3. Basic Host Discovery

   • Run:

   nmap -sn 192.168.1.0/24

   • This performs a ping scan to identify live hosts in the subnet.

4. Port Scanning

   • Run:

   nmap -p 1-1000 192.168.1.10

   • Scans ports 1 to 1000 on the target IP to find open ports.

5. Service and Version Detection

   • Run:

   nmap -sV 192.168.1.10

   • Detects services running on open ports and their versions.

6. Operating System Detection

   • Run:

   nmap -O 192.168.1.10

   • Attempts to identify the operating system of the target.

7. Aggressive Scan (Optional)

   • Run:

   nmap -A 192.168.1.10

   • Combines OS detection, version detection, script scanning, and traceroute.

8. Document Results

   • Save output using:

   nmap -oN scan_results.txt 192.168.1.10

OBSERVATIONS

Target IP       Open Ports    Services       OS Detected       Notes

192.168.1.10    22, 80, 443   SSH, HTTP,     Linux (Ubuntu)    Web server and
                              HTTPS                            SSH active

192.168.1.15    139, 445      SMB            Windows 10        File sharing enabled

192.168.1.20    None          --             --                Host unreachable
                                                                 or offline

RESULT

Nmap successfully scanned the target network and revealed active hosts, open ports, running services, and operating systems. The experiment demonstrated how Nmap can be used for network mapping, vulnerability assessment, and security auditing. It is a vital tool for ethical hacking and system administration.


