EXPERIMENT 6
PACKET ANALYSIS USING WIRESHARK
==================================================

AIM

To capture and analyze network packets using Wireshark in order to understand the structure and behavior of network protocols such as HTTP, TCP, and DNS.

DESCRIPTION

Wireshark is a powerful tool used to monitor and inspect data packets transmitted over a network. This experiment involves capturing live traffic, filtering specific protocols, and analyzing packet details to understand how data is transmitted between devices.

PROCEDURE

1. Install Wireshark

   • Download and install Wireshark from https://www.wireshark.org.
   • Ensure you have administrative privileges to capture packets.

2. Start Packet Capture

   • Launch Wireshark.
   • Select the active network interface (e.g., Ethernet or Wi-Fi).
   • Click on the blue shark fin icon to start capturing packets.

3. Generate Network Traffic

   • Open a web browser and visit a few websites (e.g., https://example.com).
   • Perform actions like DNS lookups, file downloads, or login attempts to generate diverse traffic.

4. Apply Filters

   • Use filters to isolate specific protocols:

      http – to view HTTP traffic

      tcp – to view TCP packets

      dns – to view DNS queries

   • Enter the filter in the top bar and press Enter.

5. Analyze Packets

   • Click on individual packets to view detailed information.
   • Expand sections like Ethernet, IP, TCP/UDP, and application layer protocols.
   • Note source and destination IPs, ports, flags, and payload data.

6. Stop Capture

   • Click the red square icon to stop capturing.
   • Save the capture file for future reference (File > Save As).

OBSERVATIONS

Protocol       Observation

HTTP           Requests and responses between browser and web server,
               including headers and status codes.

TCP            Three-way handshake (SYN, SYN-ACK, ACK), sequence
               numbers, retransmissions.

DNS            Query and response packets showing domain name resolution.

IP             Source and destination IP addresses, TTL values.

Ethernet       MAC addresses of communicating devices.

RESULT

Wireshark successfully captured and displayed network packets in real time. The analysis revealed the structure of various protocols and how data flows across the network. This experiment demonstrated how Wireshark can be used for troubleshooting, security analysis, and learning about network communication.


