EXPERIMENT 7
WEB APPLICATION VULNERABILITY ANALYSIS USING BURP SUITE
==================================================

AIM

To intercept, analyze, and manipulate HTTP/S requests and responses using Burp Suite in order to identify potential vulnerabilities in a web application.

DESCRIPTION

Burp Suite is a comprehensive platform used by security professionals to test the security of web applications. It includes tools for intercepting traffic, scanning for vulnerabilities, and manipulating requests. This experiment demonstrates how to use Burp Suite to analyze web traffic and detect issues such as insecure cookies, input validation flaws, and authentication weaknesses.

PROCEDURE

1. Install Burp Suite

   • Download Burp Suite Community Edition from https://portswigger.net/burp.
   • Install and launch the application.

2. Configure Browser Proxy

   • Set your browser to use Burp Suite's proxy:

      Proxy address: 127.0.0.1

      Port: 8080

   • Install Burp's CA certificate to avoid HTTPS warnings.

3. Start Intercepting Traffic

   • In Burp Suite, go to the Proxy tab and ensure Intercept is on.
   • Visit a test website (e.g., http://testphp.vulnweb.com) in your browser.

4. Analyze Requests

   • Observe intercepted HTTP requests.
   • Forward or drop requests as needed.
   • Send interesting requests to Repeater for manual testing.

5. Scan for Vulnerabilities

   • Right-click a request and select "Scan" (available in Burp Suite Professional).
   • In Community Edition, manually test for issues like:

      SQL injection

      Cross-site scripting (XSS)

      Broken authentication

6. Use Repeater and Intruder

   • Repeater: Modify and resend requests to test server responses.
   • Intruder: Automate attacks like brute force or fuzzing (limited in Community Edition).

7. Review Findings

   • Check the Target and HTTP history tabs for a full log of interactions.
   • Note any unusual responses, error messages, or security headers.

OBSERVATIONS

Test Action                 Server Response                  Potential Issue

Removed input validation    Server returned SQL error        Possible SQL Injection

Injected <script> tag       Reflected in response             XSS vulnerability

Modified session cookie     Access granted without login     Broken authentication

Checked headers             Missing X-Frame-Options,         Weak security
                            Content-Security-Policy          headers

RESULT

Burp Suite successfully intercepted and analyzed HTTP/S traffic between the browser and the web server. Manual testing revealed potential vulnerabilities such as XSS and weak authentication mechanisms. This experiment demonstrated how Burp Suite can be used for ethical hacking, penetration testing, and securing web applications.


